WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
<v Narrator>Welcome.

2
00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.550
You already know a lot about your work.

3
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:10.300
You know the programs, the forms, the steps and the people you work beside.

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:16.050
That knowledge matters, and nothing in this course asks you to set it aside.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.000
This course is about the moment when your knowledge meets another person's

6
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.350
experience, and the two do not quite match.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:30.900
Knowing a service well does not mean knowing what it means to every person who uses

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.250
it.

9
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.000
Cultural humility helps you keep that difference in view.

10
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:41.350
It means learning what something means to the person in front of you.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.100
It means looking honestly at the interpretation you bring.

12
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:50.050
And it means paying attention to the influence your public-service role carries.

13
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:56.600
You will look at what that involves, try a few practices, and then step into a

14
00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.350
workplace situation where your choices shape what happens next.

15
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:07.500
<v Narrator>Here is a small example that shows up in several of the readings behind this course.

16
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.250
Staff send a letter with the correct building address.

17
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.400
A person arrives and walks around the building, unable to tell which door is the

18
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:18.750
public entrance.

19
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.500
Staff checked the address.

20
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:21.850
It is right.

21
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.400
The person's experience is also real.

22
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.750
Both accounts matter, and they answer different questions.

23
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.700
The address answers where the building is.

24
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.250
The person's account answers whether someone new can actually get in.

25
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.600
When the reply is, “The directions are complete,” the person's knowledge gets set

26
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.950
aside.

27
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.300
Nothing dramatic happened.

28
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.050
No one meant harm.

29
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.400
Yet if unclear routes are always called complete, insiders get in more easily.

30
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.550
People meeting the process for the first time do not.

31
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.900
Small exchanges like this one, repeated, shape who a public service works for.

32
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:05.050
<v Narrator>Counseling psychologist Joshua Hook and three colleagues have studied humility in

33
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.400
helping relationships.

34
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.150
They describe two sides.

35
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.100
The inward side is an accurate view of yourself, including your limits.

36
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:17.650
The outward side is focusing on the other person with curiosity.

37
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.600
In their research, when clients rated their therapist as culturally humble, that

38
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.350
rating predicted a stronger working relationship and better outcomes.

39
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.900
Two cautions come with that finding.

40
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:34.650
Most of the evidence comes from therapy and relies on what clients report about

41
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.000
their experience.

42
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:41.350
Using it in case management, benefits work or policy work takes some adaptation.

43
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:47.100
The reading also notes a criticism some people raise: a focus on personal humility

44
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.050
can play down inequities built into systems.

45
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.200
It works best paired with efforts at the system level.

46
00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.550
Keep both points in mind as you go.

47
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.300
<v Narrator>Here is the path ahead.

48
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.650
First, what cultural humility is and is not, as the readings define it.

49
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.000
Then you turn toward your own assumptions, the meanings you add without noticing.

50
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:14.350
Next come practices for curiosity, listening and respect.

51
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.700
After that, what to do when it goes wrong, because sometimes it will.

52
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:26.250
Then you look at how humility fits the structured tools and real duties in DHS work.

53
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.800
Each part ends with a few questions to think over, alone or with a colleague you

54
00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.150
trust.

55
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.900
Before you go on, bring one real situation from your own work to mind.

56
00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.450
A conversation, a letter, a meeting.

57
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.200
Keep that question within easy reach as you continue.

58
00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.750
Let it travel with you through each of the remaining segments.

